{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff -- docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md 2>/dev/null'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md\nindex f6741f4c..861b0635 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md\n@@ -3184,31 +3184,27 @@ restarts from its carried sequence; a successor carries the next sequence.\n Durable input records preserve read outcomes, correlation results, gap\n successors, and their ordering for deterministic recovery of that interval.\n \n-The following is a proposed UCF amendment for M-next, not an existing\n-version-0 field. Publish it before implementation accepts it:\n-\n-```clojure\n-;; in the continuation's :yin.k/scheduler\n-:yin.k/next-op-seq 0\n-;; in a retained effect's :yin.k/pending, when already assigned\n-:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}\n-\n-;; composition's protected writer envelope, outside program data\n-{:yin.k/envelope :yin.k/fenced-v1\n- :yin.k/incarnation lease-id\n- :yin.k/epoch e\n- :yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}\n- :yin.k/value payload}\n-\n-;; the enrolled consumer's admission outcome, appended to the\n-;; composition stream; closed dispatch key, exactly these values,\n-;; each carrying its op id (carries as 14.2.3 states):\n-;;   :committed :replayed :stale :intent-conflict :suspended\n-\n-;; epoch and op-id sequence are nonnegative portable exact\n-;; integers bounded by 2^52-1; overflow suspends admission and\n-;; export and never wraps\n-```\n+The wire grammar for this contract is published as the UCF version-1\n+amendment (M-next B) and is not restated here:\n+\n+- UCF 7.2.1: the handoff body, its `:yin.k/version`, the version gate,\n+  and the custody header. `:yin.k/next-op-seq` is a top-level header\n+  key beside `:yin.k/id-counter`, because the handoff body flattens\n+  the scheduler slice.\n+- UCF 7.4.3: `:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` on a\n+  retained write's pending, the explicit-park shape, and the complete\n+  install child.\n+- UCF 7.7.8: the sequence state, the `:yin.k/fenced-v1` envelope, the\n+  grant epoch binding (`:yin.k/custody :yin.k/bound`), the admission\n+  check order, and the 2^52-1 bound with its overflow behavior.\n+- UCF 7.9: the closed `:yin.k/admission` outcomes.\n+\n+Implementation accepts only what those sections publish. Two points\n+were settled there and bind this section: a carried id names the\n+occurrence it was assigned under, so admission checks tenure on the\n+envelope's lease binding and accepts an id of that occurrence or of\n+an ancestor in the same chain; and an outcome echoes the envelope's\n+incarnation beside its op id.\n \n The authority publishes an attributed grant binding containing occurrence,\n lease, holder, and `:yin.k/epoch`, alongside the unchanged DaoLease fact.\n@@ -3270,7 +3266,8 @@ collection requires authoritative closure and permanent replay rejection.\n    cannot be repaired by reading carrier history.\n \n Consumer admission outcomes are composition stream data, not new UCF\n-statuses or link refusals. For M-next their closed dispatch key is\n+statuses or link refusals; UCF 7.9 publishes their grammar and the\n+keys each carries. For M-next their closed dispatch key is\n `:yin.k/admission`, with values `:committed`, `:replayed`, `:stale`,\n `:intent-conflict`, and `:suspended`. Each carries op id; committed/replayed\n carry the recorded result. Stale carries observed epoch/lease state,\n@@ -3340,6 +3337,10 @@ stubs. Also run the ordered cross-host candidate pairs of 14.1.1.\n    is absent, amend UCF 7.4.3 first; never export the name-only install\n    sketch as complete task state. Update 7.11's evidence pointers without\n    reopening or silently reassigning the existing M4 kept-cursor gate.\n+   Published 2026-10-04 as UCF amendment r5: 7.2.1, 7.4.3, 7.7.8, 7.9,\n+   and the version-1 block of 7.11.1. Both 7.4.3 shapes were absent\n+   from the UCF text and are amended there. This is a document\n+   change: it lands no code and closes no gate.\n 3. M-next C implements durable authority transactions, attribution,\n    reclaim epochs, op-id/intent/result records, input replay, and completion\n    eligibility. Prove atomicity and reopen tests before enabling enrolled\n@@ -3354,9 +3355,15 @@ stubs. Also run the ordered cross-host candidate pairs of 14.1.1.\n    consumers pass their gates. Record per-host results and any unsupported\n    composition; do not claim full UCF closure from milestone completion.\n \n+Status: M-next A is landed (80b59233). M-next B is the published\n+amendment above. M-next C, D, and E remain, and with them every test\n+contract of 14.2.4; nothing here claims full UCF closure.\n+\n Sequence/pending fields change the accepted UCF envelope grammar: publish\n `:yin.k/version 1` for this amendment and refuse unsupported versions with\n-profile-mismatch before restoration. Version 0 remains usable under its\n+profile-mismatch before restoration (published: UCF 7.2.1; the version\n+raised is the handoff body's own top-level key, not the code stamp's).\n+Version 0 remains usable under its\n published contract for fork; it is not silently upgraded to fenced custody.\n Code stamps v3/b2/r2 and module manifest schema 1 stay unchanged because\n this amendment changes handoff data and composition admission, not opcode\ndiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\nindex e38c851f..d40bad36 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n@@ -34,6 +34,12 @@ contract stamp moves to \"v3\" because the resolution order refuses the\n reserved name before env and a `:define` transition is added (S7.3.3);\n `yin/def` leaves the primitive registry (S7.5.2) and the free-name set\n (S7.6.1); S7.11 records the remaining definition-frame obligations.\n+Amended 2026-10-04 (r5) for fenced custody (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3,\n+M-next B): the handoff body is published as wire grammar, and\n+`:yin.k/version 1` adds the custody header, the operation-sequence state,\n+the fenced envelope, the grant epoch binding, and the admission outcomes\n+(7.2.1, 7.4.3, 7.7.8, 7.9, 7.11.1). The amendment is published design:\n+no version-1 code is landed and it closes no blocker.\n Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md); builds on the Phase 0\n contract in [`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) (§1–§4), the storage\n contract in [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md), the stream contract in\n@@ -141,6 +147,138 @@ stream outcome is involved, it is preserved unchanged under its own dispatch\n key `:dao.stream/outcome` — UCF never renames or nests it under a private\n key. The full algebra is in §7.9.\n \n+### 7.2.1 The handoff body: the task wire grammar, versions 0 and 1\n+\n+(Amendment r5, 2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2.) The\n+envelope above is the design shape. The value a driver publishes is the\n+**handoff body**: one task, the whole blocked machine of 7.6.3, as one\n+map in canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) under its content address.\n+Where the envelope sketch and this grammar differ, this grammar is the\n+wire contract. A task has an ordered wait set, so the body carries\n+ordered `:yin.k/frames`, never one `:yin.k/frame`. The scheduler slice of\n+7.6.3 is flattened into top-level keys. Code travels as an address-keyed\n+map. The body carries no `:yin.k/id`: its address is the hash of the body\n+(7.3.4). A halt is a body of kind `:halted`, the `:yin.k/result` sibling.\n+\n+```clojure\n+{:yin.k/handoff       true      ; the tag\n+ :yin.k/version       0         ; the BODY's version: 0 or 1\n+ :yin.k/kind          :blocked  ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n+ :yin.k/contract      {:yin.code/contract \"v3\" :yin.k/version 0}\n+ :yin.k/id-counter    n         ; 7.6.3 fresh-name state\n+ :yin.k/parked        {pid registers}      ; 7.6.3 parked records\n+ :yin.k/store         {encoded encoded}    ; 7.6.2 isolated slice\n+ :yin.k/module-stores {module {encoded encoded}}\n+ :yin.k/cells         {cell {:yin.k/stream marker\n+                             :yin.k/position p}}   ; 7.5.3\n+ :yin.k/requires      {:yin.k/cursor-profiles #{profile}\n+                       :yin.k/segments #{address}}\n+ :yin.k/code          {address vector}     ; 7.3.4, each verified\n+ ;; by kind\n+ :yin.k/frames        [{:yin.k/registers registers\n+                        :yin.k/pending pending}]   ; in wait order\n+ :yin.k/parked-id     pid       ; :parked only: the active record\n+ :yin.k/result        encoded   ; :halted only\n+ ;; when the task holds live install children (7.4.3)\n+ :yin.k/installs      {module install}}\n+```\n+\n+`registers` is `{:yin.k/segment address :yin.k/pc n :yin.k/env {name\n+encoded} :yin.k/stack [encoded] :yin.k/k [frame]}`, every key required,\n+the segment a key of `:yin.k/code` and the pc inside that vector.\n+`pending` is one variant of 7.4.3. Every value is in the 7.5 grammar.\n+\n+Shape rules, each a `:yin.k/undecodable` refusal naming its path:\n+\n+- `:blocked` requires nonempty frames. `:halted` requires\n+  `:yin.k/result` and forbids frames. `:parked` requires\n+  `:yin.k/parked-id` naming a key of `:yin.k/parked`. A `:yin.k/result`\n+  on any other kind is refused.\n+- Frames restore in their carried order, for `:blocked` and for\n+  `:parked` alike; a resumer never drops a carried frame (7.4.3).\n+- A frame's resume pc is a static safepoint whose kinds admit the\n+  pending's reason; a parked record's resume pc is an `:explicit-park`\n+  safepoint (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8).\n+- The cells the body carries are exactly the cells its roots name:\n+  none missing, none extra. A body with cells claims\n+  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n+- Each code vector hashes to its key (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch`) and is\n+  well formed under the stamp.\n+\n+Lift adds these kinds to the `:yin.k/non-portable` set of 7.5.4:\n+`:incomplete-install`, `:install-response`, `:incomplete-write`,\n+`:reason-mismatch`, `:inconsistent-halt`, `:unaddressed-segment`,\n+`:address-mismatch`, and, under version 1, `:unprotected-pending` and\n+`:op-seq-exhausted` (7.4.3).\n+\n+**Two version keys.** `:yin.k/version` at the top of the body is the\n+body's own version and is what this amendment raises. The key of the\n+same name inside `:yin.k/contract` belongs to the code stamp of 7.3.3.\n+That stamp is compared whole and stays `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\"\n+:yin.k/version 0}`: the amendment changes handoff data and composition\n+admission, not opcode semantics, so code stamps \"v3\", \"b2\" and \"r2\" and\n+module manifest schema 1 are unchanged, and cursor vectors keep their\n+bytes.\n+\n+**The version gate.** A resumer decodes the bytes, checks the tag, and\n+then checks the body version before any other rule. A version it does\n+not speak, an absent version, or a version that is not an integer is\n+`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, carrying `:yin.k/version` as found and\n+`:yin.k/supported`, the set the resumer speaks. The gate runs before\n+the remaining grammar, before any code hash is verified, before any\n+stream is attached, before any proposal is made, and before anything\n+is restored. An install child's body carries the version of the body\n+that holds it; a mixed tree is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n+\n+**Version 0** is the grammar above and nothing more. It is a fork: it\n+names no occurrence, proposes nothing, and runs through no fenced\n+writer. A version-0 body stays valid under this contract and is never\n+upgraded to fenced custody, by a resumer or by a carrier. A version-0\n+reader ignores keys it does not know, so a custody key inside a\n+version-0 body establishes nothing. A composition that requires\n+exclusive custody and is handed a version-0 body refuses it as\n+`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n+\n+**Version 1** is the fenced-custody grammar. A version-1 root body of\n+kind `:blocked` or `:parked` adds a custody header:\n+\n+```clojure\n+{:yin.k/version     1\n+ :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive\n+ :yin.k/occurrence  O           ; the lease subject, 7.3.4 and 7.7.2\n+ :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n+                     :dao.stream/descriptor d}     ; 7.7.3\n+ :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence P           ; the predecessor\n+                     :dao.lease/lease L            ; the grant it ran in\n+                     :yin.k/emitter a}             ; absent on first park\n+ :yin.k/next-op-seq n}          ; 7.7.8\n+```\n+\n+Header rules, each `:yin.k/undecodable` naming its path:\n+\n+- `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/arbitration` and\n+  `:yin.k/next-op-seq` are required. The policy is exactly\n+  `:yin.k/exclusive`: version 1 has no fork form, and fork uses\n+  version 0.\n+- `:yin.k/occurrence` is plain data in the canonical bytes domain,\n+  never nil, compared by its canonical bytes. Minting it is the\n+  emitter's (7.3.4); its form is not fixed here.\n+- `:yin.k/origin` is absent on a first export and required on every\n+  successor. Its occurrence differs from the body's own.\n+- `:yin.k/next-op-seq` is an exact integer of 7.7.8. It is 0 on a\n+  first export: that checkpoint is the recorded operation baseline.\n+- A version-1 `:halted` body carries `:yin.k/origin` and none of the\n+  other header keys: a result is not a lease subject, and nothing\n+  remains to admit.\n+- An install child's body carries none of the header keys. A child is\n+  part of its root's task, not a custody subject; its effects are\n+  admitted under the root's occurrence and drawn from the root's\n+  sequence.\n+\n+No body carries an epoch. A body is minted before any grant and its\n+bytes do not change on reclaim; the epoch is learned only from the\n+grant binding of 7.7.8.\n+\n ## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\n \n ### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\n@@ -693,6 +831,122 @@ restores every waiter with the required module's symbol as its value,\n `refused` with the refusal as the effect's error. On `gap` the honest\n outcome is a `gap`, and the composition's policy decides.\n \n+**Amendment r5: the shapes left open, and the keys on the wire.**\n+(2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2. The first two rules\n+hold for both body versions of 7.2.1; operation ids are version 1.)\n+\n+*Explicit park is the no-wait shape.* `:park` raises no wait entry, so\n+it has no frame and no pending. The parked record travels in\n+`:yin.k/parked` under its own id as a `registers` map, a body of kind\n+`:parked` names the active record by `:yin.k/parked-id`, and the\n+resumer re-creates the record and waits on nothing. `:park` is\n+therefore not a wire reason. Neither is `:call-effect`: an effectful\n+call carries the variant of the effect it was observed to produce\n+(`:next`, `:put`, `:ffi`, `:ffi-request`, a link variant, or\n+`:install`), by the ruling in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8. A\n+pending whose reason is `:park` or `:call-effect`, or any reason\n+outside the variants here, is `:yin.k/undecodable`. This supersedes\n+those two entries of the reason comment in 7.4.1.\n+\n+*The published `:install` variant is not complete alone.* The variant\n+above names a module and nothing else. The install it waits on is a\n+child task on the emitter's scheduler (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3): a\n+verified image, a phase, a store, and waits of its own. None of that\n+is in the name. A resumer handed only the name can do one of two\n+things, and both are wrong: wait forever on a child nobody runs, or\n+request the link again, which is a second effect the parked task\n+never made. That is the **name-only install sketch**, and it must\n+never be exported as task state. The complete shape is the pending\n+together with the body's install entry for the same name:\n+\n+```clojure\n+;; the waiter's pending: unchanged, never sufficient alone\n+{:yin.k/reason :install\n+ :yin.k/name   'foo}\n+\n+;; the body's entry for that name (7.2.1)\n+:yin.k/installs\n+{'foo {:yin.k/phase    :running   ; the child's phase (linker 7.3)\n+       :yin.k/parent   [:t0 7]    ; the link id that spawned it\n+       :yin.k/response {...}      ; the verified link response\n+       :yin.k/child    {...}}}    ; the child's whole handoff body\n+```\n+\n+- The response is what a foreign resumer spawns its own fresh child\n+  from, and what the child's exports are validated against at halt.\n+  It is carried verbatim and must be in the canonical bytes domain,\n+  else lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:install-response`.\n+- The child is a whole body of 7.2.1 in the version of its holder,\n+  with its own frames, parked records, stores, cells, code, and\n+  nested installs. It is validated by the same grammar, recursively,\n+  before anything of the parent is restored.\n+- Every live child travels, waited on or not, and no child steps\n+  while its task is exporting (7.7.4). A child that cannot be\n+  exported refuses the parent's export with the child's own outcome;\n+  a child that cannot be lowered refuses the parent's lower. No\n+  parent is published or restored over a child that did not cross.\n+- An `:install` pending whose name has no entry refuses the lift as\n+  `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:incomplete-install`, before\n+  publication; a body carrying such a pending is\n+  `:yin.k/undecodable`. An obligation a carried child will bind on\n+  completion is discharged by that child, not reported missing.\n+- The waiter list is not carried. The waiters of a child are exactly\n+  the frames whose `:install` pending names it, in frame order.\n+- `:yin.k/phase` and `:yin.k/parent` are required under version 1.\n+  A version-0 entry without a phase reads as `:running`.\n+\n+*Operation ids ride on retained writes (version 1).* Three variants\n+retain a write that may already have been attempted: `:put`,\n+`:ffi-request`, and `:link-request`. Each gains one optional key:\n+\n+```clojure\n+:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}   ; 7.7.8\n+```\n+\n+- The id is present exactly when the write was first attempted\n+  through a fenced writer. It was assigned once, before that first\n+  attempt, and is carried verbatim through park, lift, transfer,\n+  lower, and every retry. Lift never assigns or renumbers an id, and\n+  lower never mints one for a carried pending.\n+- `P` is the occurrence whose grant the assigning holder ran under.\n+  A body is minted at the park that ends that run, so `P` is never\n+  the body's own occurrence: it is the origin's or an earlier link\n+  of the same chain. A body with no `:yin.k/origin` carries no id.\n+- Every carried `:yin.k/seq` is below the root's\n+  `:yin.k/next-op-seq`, and no two pendings of one task, children\n+  included, share an id.\n+- An id on any other variant, a malformed id, or a breach of the two\n+  rules above is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n+- The retry appends the retained value through the resumer's fenced\n+  writer under the carried id, with the resumer's own lease and\n+  epoch. It is never a bare append, and the wait advances only on\n+  the admission outcome correlated to that id (7.9).\n+- A retained write without an id is a write to a stream the emitter\n+  declared unprotected. If the resumer's composition enrolls that\n+  stream, or cannot fence a stream whose pending carries an id, the\n+  lower refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. Protection\n+  is neither added to an attempted write nor dropped from one.\n+- A first exclusive export of a task whose retained write targets\n+  an enrolled stream refuses the lift as `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n+  `:unprotected-pending`: an operation attempted without an id\n+  cannot be made exactly-once afterwards.\n+\n+*Required keys per variant, as the handoff body carries them.* Where\n+an example above differs, this list is the wire contract:\n+\n+- `:next`: `:yin.k/cell`.\n+- `:ffi`: `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/call-id`; `:yin.k/op` optional. The\n+  response stream is the cell's own stream marker.\n+- `:put`: `:yin.k/stream` (a stream marker), `:yin.k/value`.\n+- `:ffi-request`: `:yin.k/call-id`, `:yin.k/request-envelope` (the\n+  request, retried verbatim), `:yin.k/request`, `:yin.k/response`,\n+  `:yin.k/response-cell`; `:yin.k/request-op` and\n+  `:yin.k/request-args` are optional restatements of the envelope.\n+- `:link-request` and `:link-response`: `:yin.k/link-id`,\n+  `:yin.k/name`, `:yin.k/request`, `:yin.k/response`, `:yin.k/cell`;\n+  `:link-request` also `:yin.k/envelope`.\n+- `:install`: `:yin.k/name`, and the install entry above.\n+\n ## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\n \n ### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\n@@ -1423,6 +1677,8 @@ wrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream; the resource enforces it.\n No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\n its own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\n the bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\n+Section 7.7.8 publishes the envelope's grammar, which adds the epoch to\n+the keys named here, and the sequence state behind the operation id.\n \n **Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\n completion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\n@@ -1481,6 +1737,193 @@ occurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\n recoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\n why custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\n \n+### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\n+\n+(Amendment r5, 2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2.) This\n+section publishes the grammar that 7.7.5 argues for. The reasons stay\n+in 7.7.5; the runtime, ledger, and step contract stay in\n+`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2. Nothing here adds a key to the lease\n+vocabulary or to a DaoStream outcome map. Every rule applies to\n+version-1 bodies only (7.2.1).\n+\n+**Exact integers.** `:yin.k/epoch`, `:yin.k/seq` and\n+`:yin.k/next-op-seq` are nonnegative exact integers no greater than\n+2^52-1 (4503599627370495), the bound within which every host compares\n+and increments exactly (`dao.lease.md`, *Keys*, uses the same one).\n+The kind is the canonical codec's integer kind: a float is not an\n+integer even when it is integral. A value outside the range or of\n+another kind makes its carrier defective: a body is\n+`:yin.k/undecodable`, an envelope or a binding establishes nothing.\n+No counter wraps, saturates silently, or restarts.\n+\n+**Operation sequence state.** A task under a grant holds one counter,\n+`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, on its root. Install children draw from it.\n+\n+1. *Assign.* Before the first attempt of a write to an enrolled\n+   stream, the fenced writer takes `n`, the counter's value, forms\n+   the id `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` with `P` the\n+   occurrence its grant names, stores the id on the wait entry, and\n+   sets the counter to `n+1`. The three happen as one step over the\n+   machine value, before any append.\n+2. *Retain.* The id stays on the entry while the write is unfinished:\n+   a `full` path, an append of unknown effect, an acceptance with no\n+   admission outcome yet, or a `:suspended` outcome. A retry reuses\n+   the id and never takes another.\n+3. *Carry.* At a park the id travels in the pending (7.4.3) and the\n+   counter in the header (7.2.1). The successor's counter is the\n+   holder's counter at that park, so the sequence continues along\n+   the chain and never restarts at a new occurrence.\n+4. *Restore.* Lower sets the fresh task's counter to the carried\n+   value exactly. There is no local value to take a maximum with:\n+   the receiving task is new (contrast `:yin.k/id-counter`, 7.6.3).\n+5. *Regrant.* A holder granted the same checkpoint again starts from\n+   the same bytes, so from the same counter and the same carried\n+   ids. Re-executed writes take the same sequence numbers in the\n+   same order as long as its inputs replay; where they do not, the\n+   intent comparison below catches the divergence.\n+6. *Exhaust.* The largest assignable sequence is 2^52-2. A counter\n+   equal to 2^52-1 is exhausted: the writer assigns nothing and\n+   appends nothing, the write stays an undischarged wait, and a lift\n+   of that task refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n+   `:op-seq-exhausted`. Recovery is outside this protocol.\n+\n+**The fenced envelope.** A fenced writer wraps each value it appends\n+toward an enrolled consumer:\n+\n+```clojure\n+{:yin.k/envelope    :yin.k/fenced-v1   ; dispatch key; closed\n+ :yin.k/incarnation L                  ; the writer's :dao.lease/lease\n+ :yin.k/epoch       e                  ; from L's grant binding\n+ :yin.k/op-id       {:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}\n+ :yin.k/value       v}                 ; the program value, verbatim\n+```\n+\n+- All five keys are required. `:yin.k/op-id` has exactly its two\n+  keys. A consumer ignores other qualified keys, and they are never\n+  part of intent. An envelope that fails these rules, or dispatches\n+  on a value other than `:yin.k/fenced-v1`, is defective: it commits\n+  nothing, records nothing, and produces no admission outcome.\n+- **Inside program data: only `:yin.k/value`.** It is the value the\n+  program wrote, unchanged and uninterpreted. A program value that\n+  looks like an envelope is payload: it is wrapped like any other\n+  and never read as one.\n+- **Outside program data: everything else.** The envelope exists\n+  between the fenced writer and the enrolled boundary and nowhere\n+  else. It is never in a frame, an environment, a store, or a body;\n+  a pending carries the retained value and the id beside it, and\n+  the writer rebuilds the envelope at each retry from the id and\n+  its current binding. What the consumer's commit makes visible\n+  downstream is the consumer's contract; UCF requires only that the\n+  payload it commits is `:yin.k/value`.\n+- The envelope names no target, no effect kind, and no intent. The\n+  consumer takes the target identity and the effect kind from the\n+  boundary the envelope arrived at, and computes intent itself.\n+- The envelope carries no credential. The holder is authenticated\n+  by the attribution the composition supplies (`dao.lease.md`,\n+  *Composition duties*). An envelope whose attributed author is not\n+  the holder that the binding of `L` names is a fact about that\n+  author: it is defective in the sense above.\n+\n+**Intent.** Intent is the vector `[effect-kind target-identity\n+payload]`: the keyword naming the boundary's operation, the target's\n+`:dao.stream/identity`, and the envelope's `:yin.k/value`. This\n+amendment enrolls one effect kind, `:yin.k/append`, a stream append;\n+every write the reference machine retains is one. Two intents are\n+equal when their canonical bytes are equal. FFI and link correlation\n+ids are inside the payload and are compared with it; they are not\n+operation ids.\n+\n+**The grant epoch binding.** The grantor publishes one more custody\n+fact (7.7.2), authored by itself:\n+\n+```clojure\n+{:yin.k/custody    :yin.k/bound\n+ :yin.k/occurrence O\n+ :dao.lease/lease  L\n+ :dao.lease/holder H\n+ :yin.k/epoch      e}\n+```\n+\n+- The lease fact is unchanged. The binding is a separate fact, so a\n+  lease judge, which switches on the lease keys, ignores it.\n+- Unlike the offer and the resumed report, the binding is authority,\n+  because the grantor authors it. It is valid only when its\n+  attributed author is the transactor of the space the body's\n+  `:yin.k/arbitration` names; when it was admitted in the same\n+  transaction as a `:dao.lease/accepted` fact with the same lease,\n+  the same holder, and the subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}`; and when\n+  it is the only binding for `L`. Any other binding establishes\n+  nothing, and a grant on an occurrence with no valid binding\n+  confers no fenced tenure.\n+- **How a reader learns the epoch.** Only from a valid binding. A\n+  holder reads it beside its grant before it activates, and stamps\n+  it into its envelopes. A consumer reads the authority's current\n+  state inside its admission transition. An epoch a holder\n+  advertises, in an envelope or anywhere else, is a claim to be\n+  checked, never a source.\n+- **The counter.** Each occurrence has one epoch in its ledger\n+  (7.7.3). It is 0 when the occurrence is first admitted. Every\n+  reclaim of a lease on the occurrence, whatever its cause, raises\n+  it by exactly one in the transaction that records the\n+  `:dao.lease/lapsed` fact, before any new grant. A grant binds the\n+  epoch current at that grant, so the first grant binds 0 and the\n+  grant after `k` reclaims binds `k`. A successor is a new\n+  occurrence and starts at 0.\n+- **Restart.** The epoch never decreases, is never reset, and is\n+  never reused. A grantor that restarts recovers the epoch from its\n+  durable ledger and reclaims, which raises it, before it grants\n+  (7.7.7). A grantor that cannot recover the epoch grants nothing\n+  for that occurrence: 7.7.7's reclaim-and-regrant presumes the\n+  epoch survived. A new authority never starts an old occurrence at\n+  0. Recovery from lost epoch or dedup state is governance, outside\n+  this protocol (7.7.5).\n+- **Exhaustion.** A reclaim at epoch 2^52-1 still records the lapse\n+  and ends the tenure, and leaves the epoch unchanged. The\n+  occurrence then receives no further grant, and its admissions\n+  answer `:suspended`. The lease check below still refuses the old\n+  holder.\n+- The binding is not a cache of a query. `:dao.lease/lapsed` does\n+  not cross to a remote holder (`dao.lease.md`, *Carriage*) and a\n+  count of lapses does not survive a ledger restart, so the epoch\n+  is the grantor's own datum and the binding its only publication.\n+\n+**Admission.** An enrolled consumer admits each well-formed envelope\n+by one atomic transition against the authority's admission resource\n+(7.7.5). The checks run in this order and the first to fail decides\n+the outcome (7.9):\n+\n+1. *Authority.* The authority the consumer is enrolled with must be\n+   readable. If it is unreachable, if its ledger for the occurrence\n+   that `L` is bound to is not recoverable, or if that occurrence's\n+   epoch is exhausted, the outcome is `:suspended`.\n+2. *Binding.* `L` has a valid binding, and the envelope's attributed\n+   author is the holder it names. Otherwise the envelope is\n+   defective and there is no outcome.\n+3. *Tenure.* The bound occurrence is open, `L` is its active lease,\n+   and `e` equals its current epoch, else `:stale`. Staleness is\n+   decided before the dedup record is consulted: a stale envelope is\n+   refused even when a record for its id exists.\n+4. *Scope.* The id's occurrence `P` is the bound occurrence or an\n+   ancestor of it, where `Q` is the parent of `R` when the ledger\n+   records `Q` closed with successor `R`. Ancestry is a query over\n+   those completion records; no ancestry index is stored. An id\n+   outside the chain makes the envelope defective.\n+5. *Dedup.* With a record for the id: equal intent answers\n+   `:replayed` with the recorded result and commits nothing;\n+   different intent answers `:intent-conflict` and commits nothing.\n+   With no record: the effect, its result, and the record\n+   `{op-id -> {intent result}}` commit together, and the outcome is\n+   `:committed`.\n+\n+The recorded result is the effect's terminal outcome at commitment,\n+as plain data; for a stream append it is the target's DaoStream\n+outcome map, unchanged. A non-terminal outcome is not a commitment:\n+backpressure is the writer path's own `:dao.stream/full`, nothing is\n+recorded, and no admission outcome is produced. Because scope reaches\n+up the chain, a successor's body names the same arbitration medium as\n+its predecessor; the authority's completion transition refuses a\n+successor that names another.\n+\n ## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n \n **Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n@@ -1572,6 +2015,67 @@ deleted v1 `dao.stream.apply` vocabulary. Every lower failure names its\n cleanup obligation: before custody, none; after custody, release\n (§7.8 step 7).\n \n+**Amendment r5: version-1 refusals use the statuses above.** The\n+amendment adds no `:yin.k/status` value; the set stays closed.\n+\n+- An unsupported, absent, or non-integer body version is\n+  `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, carrying `:yin.k/version` as found and\n+  `:yin.k/supported`; it is raised before restoration and before\n+  every other check but the bytes and the tag (7.2.1).\n+- A malformed custody header, operation id, or install entry is\n+  `:yin.k/undecodable` with its path (7.2.1, 7.4.3).\n+- Lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` with kind `:incomplete-install`,\n+  `:unprotected-pending`, or `:op-seq-exhausted` (7.4.3, 7.7.8).\n+- A stream whose protection the resumer cannot match is\n+  `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream (7.4.3).\n+- A grant observed without a valid epoch binding is\n+  `:yin.k/not-holder`, carrying the lease state observed; the\n+  resumer releases the lease, as after any failure past custody.\n+\n+**Amendment r5: admission outcomes.** The outcome of an enrolled\n+consumer's admission (7.7.8) is data that the consumer appends to the\n+composition stream the holder's driver reads. It is one map\n+dispatching on a second key, `:yin.k/admission`. It is not a\n+`:yin.k/status` and not a link refusal: it reports what a consumer\n+did with one effect, where a status reports what a lift or a lower\n+did with a value. The two keys never appear in one map. The set is\n+closed, with exactly these five values:\n+\n+| `:yin.k/admission` | Meaning | Also carries |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| `:committed` | committed now, once | `:yin.k/effect-result` |\n+| `:replayed` | committed before; nothing new | `:yin.k/effect-result` |\n+| `:stale` | tenure is not current | `:yin.k/observed-epoch` |\n+| `:intent-conflict` | same id, other intent | both intents |\n+| `:suspended` | authority cannot decide | `:yin.k/arbitration` |\n+\n+- Every outcome carries `:yin.k/op-id`, the id of the envelope it\n+  answers, and echoes that envelope's `:yin.k/incarnation`. A driver\n+  correlates on the pair, so an outcome answering another holder's\n+  retry of the same id is not mistaken for its own.\n+- `:yin.k/effect-result` is the recorded result of 7.7.8, the same\n+  data on the first answer and on every replay. A DaoStream outcome\n+  inside it is the map the target produced, with no key added,\n+  removed, or renamed.\n+- `:stale` carries `:yin.k/observed-epoch`, the occurrence's current\n+  epoch; `:yin.k/observed-lease`, its active lease, when one exists;\n+  and `:yin.k/closed true` when the occurrence is closed.\n+- `:intent-conflict` carries `:yin.k/recorded-intent` and\n+  `:yin.k/observed-intent`, each an intent vector of 7.7.8.\n+- `:suspended` carries `:yin.k/arbitration`, naming the authority\n+  that could not be read or could not decide, as a body names it\n+  (7.2.1).\n+- Only `:committed` and `:replayed` discharge a wait, and the\n+  effect's resume value comes from their recorded result as 7.4.1\n+  states for the variant. `:suspended` leaves the wait undischarged\n+  and the id retained; the driver retries the same envelope.\n+  `:stale` and `:intent-conflict` leave the wait undischarged and\n+  end the run: the driver emits nothing further through its fenced\n+  writers and publishes no successor.\n+- A duplicate outcome for a wait already discharged is skipped, as\n+  a duplicate link response is (7.4.3). Outcomes are compared as\n+  data on every host, never as text.\n+\n ## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n \n | Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n@@ -1854,3 +2358,95 @@ claim that all five blockers have closed.\n   parked records and fresh-name state, and all missing-dependency modes\n   close in post-M5 hardening. M2 format records verify link payloads;\n   they do not prove the task's transitive runtime closure.\n+\n+**Fenced custody, version 1 (7.2.1, 7.4.3, 7.7.8, 7.9; amendment r5).**\n+\n+This block adds obligations; it changes no row above. The safepoint\n+and portable-encoding rows keep their M4 kept-cursor and reflection\n+gate with its meaning and its evidence as written. The stage-1\n+handoff tests that landed with M-next A (80b59233,\n+`test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_test.cljc`) are recorded by\n+`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.3 and by `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` I-5;\n+they are not moved here and nothing below relies on them as custody\n+evidence. The ownership row keeps its own contract; the rows below\n+are the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n+\n+- Invariant: A version-1 body, envelope, binding, and admission\n+  outcome mean the same on JVM, Node, and Dart, and every breach of\n+  their grammar is a data outcome before a machine is restored or an\n+  effect is committed.\n+- What M-next B claims: the grammar is published. It lands no code,\n+  passes no test, and closes no row. `handoff-version` is still 0.\n+- How each clause is tested on three hosts: by canonical byte\n+  fixtures decoded on each host, so a rule about integer kind or key\n+  presence is checked on the bytes and not on a host number type;\n+  and by the ordered host pairs of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.1\n+  where a value crosses. Outcomes are compared as data.\n+\n+Clauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n+(`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3):\n+\n+1. Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures: version 2, absent, a float `1.0`,\n+   a version-0 child in a version-1 root. Assert\n+   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` with the found and supported versions,\n+   or `:yin.k/undecodable` for the mixed tree, with zero attach\n+   calls, zero proposals, and no machine. A version-0 body still\n+   lowers as a fork on a resumer that speaks both; a composition\n+   requiring exclusive refuses it. Stage: D.\n+2. Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures omit each required key, give a\n+   fork policy, a nil occurrence, an origin equal to the body's\n+   occurrence, header keys on a child, and header keys other than\n+   the origin on a halted body. Assert `:yin.k/undecodable` with\n+   the path. Stage: D.\n+3. Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n+   retention across `full`, unknown effect, and `:suspended`; the\n+   counter restored exactly; children drawing from the root; a\n+   regrant of one checkpoint reassigning equal ids. Stage: D, and\n+   E across the host pairs.\n+4. Carried ids (7.4.3). An id crosses park, bytes, and lower\n+   unchanged on `:put`, `:ffi-request`, and `:link-request`, and the\n+   retry is fenced. Fixtures: an id on `:next`, a sequence at or\n+   above the counter, a duplicate id, an id in a body without an\n+   origin, an id naming the body's own occurrence. Assert\n+   `:yin.k/undecodable`. Protection mismatch in each direction is\n+   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream; a first exclusive export\n+   over an attempted write is `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D.\n+5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3). The version-0\n+   fork shape is exercised by the stage-1 tests named above. Owed\n+   for version 1: a `:park` or `:call-effect` reason and an\n+   `:install` pending without its entry refuse at decode; phase and\n+   parent are required; a child's carried ids belong to the root's\n+   sequence; a `:parked` body's frames restore in order. Stage: D.\n+6. Envelope (7.7.8). Fixtures drop each key, change the dispatch\n+   value, and nest an envelope-shaped program value. Assert the\n+   defective forms commit nothing, record nothing, and answer\n+   nothing; the nested value arrives as payload; no envelope key\n+   appears in any body. Stage: C for the consumer, D for the writer.\n+7. Epoch binding (7.7.8). First grant binds 0; each reclaim raises\n+   the epoch by one in the lapse's transaction; a successor starts\n+   at 0. A binding by another author, outside the grant's\n+   transaction, duplicated for one lease, or with a float epoch\n+   establishes nothing, and a holder that sees only such a binding\n+   answers `:yin.k/not-holder` and releases. Reopen the ledger and\n+   assert the epoch is recovered and raised, never reset. Stage: C.\n+8. Admission order and outcomes (7.7.8, 7.9). One fixture per check\n+   in order: unreadable authority, wrong author, stale epoch, stale\n+   lease, closed occurrence, an id outside the chain, an inherited\n+   id from a closed ancestor, equal intent, different intent, fresh\n+   commit. Assert the exact outcome map of 7.9 for each, that stale\n+   wins over an existing record, and that the driver discharges\n+   only on `:committed` or `:replayed` matched on id and\n+   incarnation. Stage: C for the consumer, D for the driver.\n+9. Bounds (7.7.8). A counter at 2^52-1 assigns nothing and refuses\n+   export; an epoch at 2^52-1 records the lapse, grants nothing, and\n+   answers `:suspended`; the values 2^52 and -1 are refused on the\n+   bytes. Stage: C for the epoch, D for the sequence.\n+10. Composition (linker 14.2.4). The crash, partition, reclaim, and\n+    successor-completion contracts run through the wired handoff\n+    composition on both host matrices. Stage: E.\n+\n+Not claimed by this amendment, and still owed by the ownership row:\n+exclusive custody itself, atomic admission, crash recovery, input\n+replay, and the `yin.repl.core` handoff composition. The authority\n+policy questions the amendment leaves open are recorded with its\n+findings, not decided here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | sed -n '3130,3305p'; rg -n 'defn|parked|install|version' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc | tail -45; rg -n 'defn.*validate-body|wait-set \\\\[\\\\]|handoff-version|incomplete-install|installs' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/*.cljc; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1915,2018p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | sed -n '3130,3305p'; rg -n 'defn|parked|install|version' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc | tail -45; rg -n 'defn.*validate-body|wait-set \\\\[\\\\]|handoff-version|incomplete-install|installs' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/*.cljc; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1915,2018p'\"","aggregated_output":"  3130\t\n  3131\tOn JVM, Node, and Dart, one occurrence O has at most one current admitted\n  3132\tholder at the possessing authority. Copies, alternate content encodings,\n  3133\tcarrier retries, and two candidates never create additional subjects.\n  3134\tAfter reclaim an old process may still compute; it cannot commit a\n  3135\tprotected effect. Each enrolled operation id and equal canonical intent\n  3136\tcommits at most once across all grants of O. Equal ids with different\n  3137\tintent commit nothing. This is an admission guarantee, not a promise that\n  3138\tunprotected external IO becomes transactional.\n  3139\t\n  3140\tExclusive requires a durable transactable arbitration space, attributed\n  3141\tgrantor/holder events, an authority that possesses the grounded resource\n  3142\tit arbitrates (UCF 7.7.3), and declared consumer enrollment. Signed module\n  3143\tprincipals and DHT nodes\n  3144\tare not custody principals by implication. The handoff composition supplies\n  3145\tthe attribution resolver and authority descriptor explicitly. Without\n  3146\tthat authority offer fork only; declare each unprotected stream's\n  3147\tat-least-once or fail-stop behavior. No lease gates stream retention.\n  3148\t\n  3149\tThe source exporting gate applies to every resumable row of 14.1.1:\n  3150\treads cannot consume, blocked writers and retained FFI/link requests cannot\n  3151\tappend, direct resume cannot restore reachable parked records, and an\n  3152\tinstall child cannot keep stepping. Freeze the entire task and its child\n  3153\twork, not only parent registers. Halt emits a result without resumption.\n  3154\tThe source must obtain a grant like any receiver to run after an offer.\n  3155\t\n  3156\t#### 14.2.2 Runtime, ledger, and wire contract\n  3157\t\n  3158\tThe handoff driver is an explicit step state with export record, occurrence,\n  3159\tcarrier progress, offer progress, proposal, observed grant, pending\n  3160\trelease, and successor progress. Persist occurrence and publication/offer\n  3161\tprogress before retry after restart. An unknown offer append is treated as\n  3162\tpossibly offered: the source stays fenced and reconciles with authority;\n  3163\tlocal abort is legal only with proof no offer was admitted. Duplicate\n  3164\toffer evidence is idempotent by occurrence and snapshot address.\n  3165\t\n  3166\tThe authority owns durable occurrence admission records: current epoch,\n  3167\tactive lease and attributed holder, offered snapshot variants, open/closed\n  3168\tstate, and recorded successor at completion. Queries derive projections\n  3169\tfrom these records; no second DHT ownership index is stored. Epoch is a\n  3170\tmonotone counter advanced atomically on reclaim, never reused after\n  3171\trestart. Initial epoch is zero; grants bind to the current epoch. Regrant\n  3172\tfollows reclaim, and closed occurrences cannot receive another grant.\n  3173\t\n  3174\tAn enrolled consumer owns durable records keyed by\n  3175\t`{:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}` containing canonical intent and result.\n  3176\tIntent is the canonical data vector `[effect-kind target-identity payload]`,\n  3177\tcomputed from the actual operation, never accepted from a holder-supplied\n  3178\thash alone. Correlation ids in FFI/link envelopes are part of payload and\n  3179\tare preserved; they do not replace operation ids. Runtime emission state\n  3180\tkeeps the next sequence and any pending operation's assigned id/intent.\n  3181\tAssign once before first append, increment once, and retain across full,\n  3182\tunknown acceptance, migration, and retry. Regrant of the same checkpoint\n  3183\trestarts from its carried sequence; a successor carries the next sequence.\n  3184\tDurable input records preserve read outcomes, correlation results, gap\n  3185\tsuccessors, and their ordering for deterministic recovery of that interval.\n  3186\t\n  3187\tThe wire grammar for this contract is published as the UCF version-1\n  3188\tamendment (M-next B) and is not restated here:\n  3189\t\n  3190\t- UCF 7.2.1: the handoff body, its `:yin.k/version`, the version gate,\n  3191\t  and the custody header. `:yin.k/next-op-seq` is a top-level header\n  3192\t  key beside `:yin.k/id-counter`, because the handoff body flattens\n  3193\t  the scheduler slice.\n  3194\t- UCF 7.4.3: `:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` on a\n  3195\t  retained write's pending, the explicit-park shape, and the complete\n  3196\t  install child.\n  3197\t- UCF 7.7.8: the sequence state, the `:yin.k/fenced-v1` envelope, the\n  3198\t  grant epoch binding (`:yin.k/custody :yin.k/bound`), the admission\n  3199\t  check order, and the 2^52-1 bound with its overflow behavior.\n  3200\t- UCF 7.9: the closed `:yin.k/admission` outcomes.\n  3201\t\n  3202\tImplementation accepts only what those sections publish. Two points\n  3203\twere settled there and bind this section: a carried id names the\n  3204\toccurrence it was assigned under, so admission checks tenure on the\n  3205\tenvelope's lease binding and accepts an id of that occurrence or of\n  3206\tan ancestor in the same chain; and an outcome echoes the envelope's\n  3207\tincarnation beside its op id.\n  3208\t\n  3209\tThe authority publishes an attributed grant binding containing occurrence,\n  3210\tlease, holder, and `:yin.k/epoch`, alongside the unchanged DaoLease fact.\n  3211\tThe binding is admitted in the same transaction as the grant; a reader\n  3212\tcannot infer it from an epoch advertised by the holder. Lease vocabulary\n  3213\tand DaoStream outcome maps gain no keys. The target stream identity and\n  3214\teffect kind come from the enrolled operation's boundary. Credentials are\n  3215\tcomposition resources, never raw host objects inside UCF program values.\n  3216\t\n  3217\tEpoch and sequence are nonnegative portable exact integers bounded by\n  3218\t2^52-1. Overflow suspends admission/export; neither wraps. A new authority\n  3219\tcannot restart an old occurrence at zero. Loss of recoverable epoch/dedup\n  3220\tstate requires fail-stop governance recovery, not automatic exclusive\n  3221\tregrant. Input and dedup records stay durable while any replay is allowed;\n  3222\tcollection requires authoritative closure and permanent replay rejection.\n  3223\t\n  3224\t#### 14.2.3 Exact handoff and admission steps\n  3225\t\n  3226\t1. Quiesce and enter exporting as in 14.1.2. Retain any already assigned\n  3227\t   op id and sequence. Validate portability and completeness. Append code\n  3228\t   and body, then offer O on the declared arbitration medium. Carrier\n  3229\t   publication alone is no offer and no grant. Retry every failed phase\n  3230\t   from its retained progress; never mint another O for this park.\n  3231\t2. A candidate checks stamp, hash, grammar, requirements, and authority\n  3232\t   attachment, then proposes for O. Awaiting proposals return\n  3233\t   `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`; competing candidates return\n  3234\t   `:yin.k/not-holder`. Only an attributed grant and matching epoch\n  3235\t   binding permit activation. Missing authority yields unsatisfied.\n  3236\t3. Restore resources, frame, ordered waits, replay inputs, and sequence in\n  3237\t   a private task, using 14.1.2. Any post-grant failure releases first;\n  3238\t   the source remains exporting. Run every protected effect through the\n  3239\t   fenced writer, including retries performed by polling and child work.\n  3240\t   Merely fencing the eventual register restore is too late.\n  3241\t4. Admission authenticates the holder and atomically checks open O, active\n  3242\t   lease, epoch, op id, and actual intent against the durable dedup record.\n  3243\t   Stale epoch or lease refuses even if a dedup record exists. A current\n  3244\t   duplicate with equal intent returns its stored result without commit;\n  3245\t   divergent intent refuses. A fresh operation commits effect, result,\n  3246\t   and dedup record in that same transaction. Never check remotely then\n  3247\t   perform an uncoordinated local side effect.\n  3248\t5. The enrolled boundary appends results from committed records; a crash\n  3249\t   before result delivery permits redelivery, not another commit. The\n  3250\t   driver advances a pending wait only on its correlated completion.\n  3251\t   Remote outbound ok, a lease stamp, and absence of lapse prove nothing\n  3252\t   about commitment. An unreachable authority suspends protected admission.\n  3253\t6. At the next safepoint or halt, publish the successor, report resumed\n  3254\t   with predecessor O and lease, then release. The authority's transaction\n  3255\t   validates that holder and successor, closes O, and records completion.\n  3256\t   A successor offer is eligible only after that predecessor completion;\n  3257\t   an orphan append/report cannot run a second branch. Closure fences the\n  3258\t   old lease. An authenticated release after failed lower returns O to\n  3259\t   offered through reclaim, not completed: no successor was reported.\n  3260\t7. Reclaim atomically invalidates tenure and advances epoch before any\n  3261\t   new grant. Regrant the last authoritative checkpoint, replaying durable\n  3262\t   inputs and op ids. If replay evidence is unavailable, fail-stop before\n  3263\t   protected effects; divergent same-id intent is an additional guard.\n  3264\t   Grantor restart recovers the durable ledger or reclaims prior tenure\n  3265\t   using recoverable epoch state before grant. Permanent ledger loss\n  3266\t   cannot be repaired by reading carrier history.\n  3267\t\n  3268\tConsumer admission outcomes are composition stream data, not new UCF\n  3269\tstatuses or link refusals; UCF 7.9 publishes their grammar and the\n  3270\tkeys each carries. For M-next their closed dispatch key is\n  3271\t`:yin.k/admission`, with values `:committed`, `:replayed`, `:stale`,\n  3272\t`:intent-conflict`, and `:suspended`. Each carries op id; committed/replayed\n  3273\tcarry the recorded result. Stale carries observed epoch/lease state,\n  3274\tintent-conflict carries expected and observed intent, and suspended names\n  3275\tthe unavailable authority. Compare these data on every host, never text.\n  3276\tOnly committed/replayed can supply an effect's success result.\n  3277\t\n  3278\t#### 14.2.4 M-next test contracts\n  3279\t\n  3280\tRun each contract with JVM, Node, and Dart as holder and as authority/\n  3281\tconsumer over the same portable transactional seam. A seam must model\n  3282\tatomic commitment and durable reopen, not separate mutable check/action\n  3283\tstubs. Also run the ordered cross-host candidate pairs of 14.1.1.\n  3284\t\n  3285\t- Setup: Two encodings of O, two candidates, and a wakeable source writer.\n  3286\t  Action: publish/offer, race proposals, poll source, direct-resume it,\n  3287\t  and tick its install child. Assert: one admitted holder; competitors\n  3288\t  await/refuse; source emits nothing. Grant source itself and assert it\n  3289\t  resumes only then. Fork without authority is explicitly labelled fork.\n  3290\t- Setup: Fail each carrier/offer append, including unknown acceptance;\n  3291\t  crash/reopen the export driver after each phase. Action: retry or abort.\n  3292\t  Assert: one O, retained waits/ids, no source effects, duplicate evidence\n  3293\t  creates no second grant. Abort restores local execution only before a\n  3294\t  proven unadmitted offer; uncertain/admitted offers remain fenced.\n  3295\t- Setup: Grant epoch e, delay one protected effect, reclaim and grant e+1.\n  3296\t  Action: deliver the old effect and replay the new holder's equal id and\n  3297\t  intent twice. Assert: old effect is stale; one commit and stored result\n  3298\t  for the current holder. Concurrent reclaim/admission serializes as\n  3299\t  either commit-before-reclaim or refusal-after-reclaim, never both.\n  3300\t- Setup: Crash immediately before/after atomic effect commitment and\n  3301\t  before result append; retain durable intent/result records. Action:\n  3302\t  reopen/regrant and retry. Assert: zero or one commit as appropriate,\n  3303\t  redelivered recorded result, stable ids, and no duplicate side effect.\n  3304\t  External IO outside the transaction earns no exactly-once assertion.\n  3305\t- Setup: Evict a kept-cursor value after a holder crash. Action: recover\n43:   (`yin.vm.semantic.md` S2.4); `:yin.k/version` is this envelope's own\n44:   version. A resumer on another revision answers `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`\n47:   :yin.k/version 0})\n81:(defn- index-batch\n93:(defn refusal\n104:(defn- refuse\n110:(defn- resolved-batch\n130:(defn- check-segment-entity\n139:(defn- canonical-tuple\n176:(defn batch->canonical-instruction-vector\n203:(defn code-address\n210:(defn canonicalize\n243:   move control (a `:resume` restores a parked configuration, so its\n245:   leave the machine parked with a canonical configuration at pc+1 -- the\n281:   off the parked record and wait entry by the lift driver\n290:(defn stack-effect\n305:(defn- successors\n322:(defn lexically-required\n349:(defn safepoints\n372:(defn safepoint-table\n382:(defn safepoint-at\n390:(defn activation-state\n391:  \"The dynamic half of a parked configuration, read off a frame -- a wait\n392:   entry or parked record `{segment pc env stack k}` -- never derived from\n401:(defn unsatisfied-names\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:483:   lower installs nothing at them but what the engine's own dispatch\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:25:   restoration passes.  A failed lower installs nothing partially\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:60:(def handoff-version\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:497:    (do (when-not (contains? (:installs vm) (:name entry))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:498:          (non-portable! :incomplete-install {:yin.k/name (:name entry)}))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:533:(defn- export-installs\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:551:        (:installs vm)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:631:                                      (:installs vm)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:679:               installs (export-installs vm serve!)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:699:                             :yin.k/version handoff-version\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:711:                      (seq installs)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:712:                      (assoc :yin.k/installs installs)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:931:(defn validate-body\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:942:  (when-not (= handoff-version (:yin.k/version body))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:996:    (doseq [[m inst] (:yin.k/installs body)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1001:                       :yin.k/path [:yin.k/installs m]}))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1144:(defn- resume-installs\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1171:        (:yin.k/installs body)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1327:             ;; the isolated slice installs through `store-put`, so a\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1350:             installs (resume-installs recv' body attach! opts)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1364:                                :installs (merge (:installs recv') installs)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1384:                       :wait-set [])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1390:                       :wait-set [])\n  1915\t   `{op-id -> {intent result}}` commit together, and the outcome is\n  1916\t   `:committed`.\n  1917\t\n  1918\tThe recorded result is the effect's terminal outcome at commitment,\n  1919\tas plain data; for a stream append it is the target's DaoStream\n  1920\toutcome map, unchanged. A non-terminal outcome is not a commitment:\n  1921\tbackpressure is the writer path's own `:dao.stream/full`, nothing is\n  1922\trecorded, and no admission outcome is produced. Because scope reaches\n  1923\tup the chain, a successor's body names the same arbitration medium as\n  1924\tits predecessor; the authority's completion transition refuses a\n  1925\tsuccessor that names another.\n  1926\t\n  1927\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n  1928\t\n  1929\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n  1930\t\n  1931\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n  1932\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  1933\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n  1934\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n  1935\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n  1936\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n  1937\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n  1938\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n  1939\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n  1940\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n  1941\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n  1942\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n  1943\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n  1944\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n  1945\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n  1946\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n  1947\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n  1948\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n  1949\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n  1950\t   data.\n  1951\t\n  1952\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n  1953\t\n  1954\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  1955\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n  1956\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n  1957\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n  1958\t   both checks always run.\n  1959\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n  1960\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n  1961\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n  1962\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n  1963\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n  1964\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n  1965\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n  1966\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n  1967\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1968\t   *Authority*).\n  1969\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n  1970\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n  1971\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n  1972\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n  1973\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n  1974\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n  1975\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n  1976\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n  1977\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n  1978\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n  1979\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n  1980\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n  1981\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n  1982\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n  1983\t   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\n  1984\t\n  1985\tNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\n  1986\tcomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\n  1987\tsystem.\n  1988\t\n  1989\t## §7.9 Outcome algebra\n  1990\t\n  1991\tEvery UCF operation returns one map dispatching on `:yin.k/status`; the set\n  1992\tis closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries the data needed to act on it.\n  1993\t\n  1994\t| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\n  1995\t|---|---|---|\n  1996\t| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\n  1997\t| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\n  1998\t| `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | lift | the ready-queue depth |\n  1999\t| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` — kinds at §7.5.4, cycles included as `:kind :cyclic` |\n  2000\t| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\n  2001\t| `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` | lower | the address claimed and the address computed |\n  2002\t| `:yin.k/undecodable` | lower | `:yin.k/path`, the structural defect — malformed markers, missing or cyclic refs, malformed-but-correctly-hashed bodies |\n  2003\t| `:yin.k/unsatisfied` | lower | `:yin.k/missing`, `:yin.k/discovery` — includes attachment failures, cursor profiles, and an unreachable arbitration medium |\n  2004\t| `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the proposal — a step status while a proposal is unanswered, bounded by nothing (`dao.lease.md`) |\n  2005\t| `:yin.k/not-holder` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the lease state observed |\n  2006\t\n  2007\tTerminal DaoStream outcomes met during lift or lower (`transport-error`,\n  2008\t`closed`, `invalid-descriptor`, …) are surfaced unchanged inside the UCF\n  2009\toutcome under their own key `:dao.stream/outcome`, exactly as the transport\n  2010\tproduced them; UCF does not rename them, nest them under private keys, or\n  2011\ttranslate between them. FFI requests and responses inside a value use the\n  2012\treference machine's versioned envelope, `dao.stream.apply` —\n  2013\t`:dao.stream.apply/id`, `/op`, `/args`, `/ok`, `/error` — never the\n  2014\tdeleted v1 `dao.stream.apply` vocabulary. Every lower failure names its\n  2015\tcleanup obligation: before custody, none; after custody, release\n  2016\t(§7.8 step 7).\n  2017\t\n  2018\t**Amendment r5: version-1 refusals use the statuses above.** The\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '530,553p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '654,728p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '931,1015p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1144,1175p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1350,1396p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n '\"'^### 7.7|''^### 7.6|''^##.*6|named but|install|atomic|dedup'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | tail -35; sed -n '1,33p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '530,553p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '654,728p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '931,1015p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1144,1175p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1350,1396p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n '\"'^### 7.7|''^### 7.6|''^##.*6|named but|install|atomic|dedup'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | tail -35; sed -n '1,33p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"(declare export-task validate-body resume-task)\n\n\n(defn- export-installs\n  \"Each live install child of `vm` as its own handoff body beside the\n   response that spawned it, preserving the child's phase and waits\n   (14.1.1's :install row).  A child that cannot export itself refuses\n   the parent's export -- a refusal is thrown, never embedded -- and a\n   response outside the canonical bytes domain refuses it too.\"\n  [vm serve!]\n  (into {}\n        (map (fn [[m {:keys [vm phase parent response]}]]\n               (when-not (cbor/portable-value? response)\n                 (non-portable! :install-response {:yin.k/name m}))\n               (let [r (export-task vm serve!)]\n                 (when (not= :ok (:status r))\n                   (refuse! (:yin.k/status r) (dissoc r :status)))\n                 [m {:yin.k/phase phase\n                     :yin.k/parent parent\n                     :yin.k/response response\n                     :yin.k/child (:body r)}])))\n        (:installs vm)))\n\n\n               _ (when (and (= :halted kind) (seq (:wait-set vm)))\n                   (non-portable! :inconsistent-halt {}))\n               _ (when (and active\n                            (not (some #(= :explicit-park %)\n                                       (safepoint-kinds-at\n                                         vm (:segment active-rec)\n                                         (:pc active-rec)))))\n                   (non-portable! :reason-mismatch\n                                  {:yin.k/pc (:pc active-rec)}))\n               found (atom {:cells {} :cell-of {} :markers {}\n                            :stores #{} :segments #{}})\n               encode (value-encoder vm serve! found)\n               frames (mapv (partial lift-frame! vm serve! found encode)\n                            (:wait-set vm))\n               parked (into {}\n                            (map (fn [[pid rec]]\n                                   (safepoint-kinds-at vm (:segment rec)\n                                                       (:pc rec))\n                                   [pid (encode-registers found encode\n                                                          vm rec)]))\n                            (:yin.k/parked (:yin.k/scheduler walked)))\n               store (into {}\n                           (map (fn [[k v]] [(encode k) (encode v)]))\n                           (:yin.k/store walked))\n               module-stores (snapshot-module-stores vm encode found)\n               installs (export-installs vm serve!)\n               cells (:cells @found)\n               profiles (or (:cursor-profiles opts)\n                            (if (seq cells)\n                              ucf.remote/cursor-profiles\n                              #{}))\n               _ (when (and (seq cells)\n                            (not (contains? profiles\n                                            :dao.stream.remote/v1)))\n                   (unsatisfied!))\n               segments (:segments @found)\n               fetch (completion/vm-fetch vm nil)\n               code (into {}\n                          (map (fn [a]\n                                 (if-some [v (fetch a)]\n                                   [a v]\n                                   (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied\n                                            {:yin.k/segment a}))))\n                          (sort-by str segments))\n               body (cond-> {handoff-tag true\n                             :yin.k/version handoff-version\n                             :yin.k/kind kind\n                             :yin.k/contract ucf/contract-stamp\n                             :yin.k/id-counter (or (:id-counter vm) 0)\n                             :yin.k/store store\n                             :yin.k/module-stores module-stores\n                             :yin.k/parked parked\n                             :yin.k/cells cells\n                             :yin.k/requires\n                             {:yin.k/cursor-profiles profiles\n                              :yin.k/segments (set segments)}\n                             :yin.k/code code}\n                      (seq installs)\n                      (assoc :yin.k/installs installs)\n                      (seq frames)\n                      (assoc :yin.k/frames frames)\n                      (= :parked kind)\n                      (assoc :yin.k/parked-id (:id value))\n                      (= :halted kind)\n                      (assoc :yin.k/result (encode (:value vm))))\n               bytes (cbor/encode body)\n               _ (validate-body (cbor/decode bytes))]\n           {:status :ok\n            :kind kind\n            :body body\n            :bytes bytes\n            :address (jing/content-hash body)}))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n(defn validate-body\n  \"The body grammar both ends run (S7.5.4): the tag, the version, the\n   kind's own shape, every cell reference resolved to exactly the\n   cells the body carries -- none missing, none extra -- every pending\n   a known variant with the keys that variant requires, every frame's\n   segment among the code the body names, and each code vector still\n   hashing to its own address and admissible as a vector.  A malformed\n   body is refused whole, before any attachment or restoration.\"\n  [body]\n  (when-not (and (map? body) (true? (get body handoff-tag)))\n    (undecodable! {:yin.k/kind :body}))\n  (when-not (= handoff-version (:yin.k/version body))\n    (refuse! :yin.k/profile-mismatch\n             {:yin.k/version (:yin.k/version body)}))\n  (let [kind (:yin.k/kind body)\n        cells (or (:yin.k/cells body) {})\n        code (or (:yin.k/code body) {})]\n    (when-not (contains? #{:blocked :parked :halted} kind)\n      (undecodable! {:yin.k/kind kind :yin.k/path [:yin.k/kind]}))\n    (when-not (and (map? cells) (map? code))\n      (undecodable! {:yin.k/path [:yin.k/cells]}))\n    (doseq [[cid cell] cells]\n      (when-not (and (map? cell) (marker? (:yin.k/stream cell))\n                     (contains? cell :yin.k/position))\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/cell cid :yin.k/path [:yin.k/cells cid]})))\n    (doseq [[a v] code]\n      (when-not (= a (ucf/code-address v))\n        (refuse! :yin.k/hash-mismatch {:yin.k/segment a}))\n      (when-some [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/segment a :yin.k/pc (:pc defect)\n                       :yin.k/defect defect})))\n    (validate-frames body code cells)\n    (when (contains? body :yin.k/frames)\n      (when (= :halted kind)\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/path [:yin.k/frames]})))\n    (when (and (= :blocked kind)\n               (not (seq (or (:yin.k/frames body) []))))\n      (undecodable! {:yin.k/path [:yin.k/frames]\n                     :yin.k/kind :blocked-without-frames}))\n    (when (contains? body :yin.k/result)\n      (when-not (= :halted kind)\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/path [:yin.k/result]})))\n    (when (= :halted kind)\n      (when-not (contains? body :yin.k/result)\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/path [:yin.k/result]})))\n    (when (= :parked kind)\n      (when-not (contains? (:yin.k/parked body)\n                           (:yin.k/parked-id body))\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/parked-id (:yin.k/parked-id body)\n                       :yin.k/path [:yin.k/parked-id]})))\n    (doseq [[pid r] (:yin.k/parked body)]\n      (let [path [:yin.k/parked pid]]\n        (validate-registers code r path)\n        ;; a parked record IS an explicit park: its resume pc must sit\n        ;; at the one safepoint kind whose meaning is the park itself,\n        ;; so a record steered onto an ordinary instruction pc never\n        ;; restores as a continuation\n        (let [at (ucf/safepoint-at (get code (:yin.k/segment r))\n                                   (:yin.k/pc r))]\n          (when (or (contains? at :yin.k/status)\n                    (not (contains? (set (:yin.safepoint/kinds at))\n                                    :explicit-park)))\n            (undecodable! {:yin.k/parked-id pid\n                           :yin.k/pc (:yin.k/pc r)\n                           :yin.k/path path})))))\n    (doseq [[m inst] (:yin.k/installs body)]\n      (when-not (and (contains? inst :yin.k/response)\n                     (map? (:yin.k/child inst))\n                     (true? (get (:yin.k/child inst) handoff-tag)))\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/name m\n                       :yin.k/path [:yin.k/installs m]}))\n      ;; a child is a whole body: validated with the same grammar\n      ;; before anything of the parent is restored.  Decoded bytes\n      ;; are trees, so the recursion terminates.\n      (validate-body (:yin.k/child inst)))\n    (let [referenced (referenced-cells body)\n          missing (remove #(contains? cells %) referenced)\n          extra (remove #(contains? referenced %) (keys cells))]\n      (when-some [cid (first missing)]\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/cell cid :yin.k/path [:yin.k/cells]}))\n      (when-some [cid (first (sort-by str extra))]\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/cell cid :yin.k/kind :extra-cell\n                       :yin.k/path [:yin.k/cells cid]})))\n    (when (and (seq cells)\n               (not (contains?\n(defn- resume-installs\n  \"Each install child resumed into a fresh child of the receiver's\n   composition, its phase and response carried: the receiver's own\n   scheduler steps it exactly as the source's did.  A child that\n   refuses -- its bytes undecodable, a stream it names unattachable\n   -- aborts the parent's restoration: the refusal is thrown here,\n   before any machine value is assembled, so the parent never answers\n   :ok over a child that did not lower.\"\n  [recv body attach! opts]\n  (into {}\n        (map (fn [[m inst]]\n               (let [response (:yin.k/response inst)\n                     child-body (:yin.k/child inst)\n                     resumed (resume-task\n                               (if-some [spawn (:child-of opts)]\n                                 (spawn recv response m)\n                                 (spawn-child-template recv response m))\n                               (cbor/encode child-body)\n                               attach!\n                               opts)]\n                 (when (not= :ok (:status resumed))\n                   (refuse! (:yin.k/status resumed)\n                            (dissoc resumed :yin.k/status)))\n                 [m {:phase (or (:yin.k/phase inst) :running)\n                     :parent (:yin.k/parent inst)\n                     :response response\n                     :vm (:vm resumed)}])))\n        (:yin.k/installs body)))\n\n\n(defn- link-pair-resources\n  \"The engine's fixed link keys mapped onto the reflections a lowered\n             installs (resume-installs recv' body attach! opts)\n             registers (mapv (fn [frame]\n                               (decode-registers decode aliases\n                                                 (:yin.k/registers frame)))\n                             (or (:yin.k/frames body) []))\n             entries (mapv (fn [pending ctx]\n                             (lower-frame! decode streams cells cell-keys\n                                           pending ctx))\n                           pendings registers)\n             machine (-> recv'\n                         (assoc :store store\n                                :module-stores (merge (:module-stores recv')\n                                                      module-stores)\n                                :parked (merge (:parked recv') parked)\n                                :installs (merge (:installs recv') installs)\n                                :resources (merge (:resources recv')\n                                                  resources\n                                                  cell-resources\n                                                  (link-pair-resources\n                                                    streams resources\n                                                    pendings))\n                                :id-counter (max (or (:id-counter recv') 0)\n                                                 (or (:yin.k/id-counter body)\n                                                     0))\n                                :ready-queue []\n                                :control nil\n                                :k nil))]\n         {:status :ok\n          :kind kind\n          :vm (case kind\n                :halted\n                (assoc machine\n                       :halted? true :blocked? false\n                       :value (decode (:yin.k/result body))\n                       :wait-set [])\n\n                :parked\n                (assoc machine\n                       :halted? true :blocked? false\n                       :value (get parked (:yin.k/parked-id body))\n                       :wait-set [])\n\n                (assoc machine\n                       :halted? false :blocked? true\n                       :value :yin/blocked\n                       :wait-set entries))})))))\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1393:manifest address: the installing child's halted store, and every\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1399:and whatever that grammar refuses refuses the lift and the install. On\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1410:### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1442:### 7.6.4 Modules\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1455:### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1485:### 7.7.1 The race, named\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1500:### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1534:### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1574:occurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1580:### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1630:### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1644:**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1648:operation-intent check, durable dedup record, and side-effect commitment\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1649:share one atomic boundary against the admission resource, never a remote\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1654:**Stable operation ids, intent, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1660:with its durable `{op-id -> {intent result}}` record in the atomic\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1692:atomic intent comparison prevent a divergent same-id effect from\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1701:delivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1714:### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1730:### 7.7.7 Restart\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1740:### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1878:  0. Recovery from lost epoch or dedup state is governance, outside\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1891:by one atomic transition against the authority's admission resource\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1904:   decided before the dedup record is consulted: a stale envelope is\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2025:- A malformed custody header, operation id, or install entry is\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2027:- Lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` with kind `:incomplete-install`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2124:- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2125:  and durable op-id plus intent dedup at enrolled consumers are design\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2126:  items for `dao.space.transactor.md`. A consumer without a shared atomic\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2224:  `:link-response`, and `:install` pending variants (linker section 11\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2319:  check, dedup record, and side-effect commitment share one atomic\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2355:  footprints, transitive child-install slices, and isolated lowering\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2414:5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3). The version-0\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2417:   `:install` pending without its entry refuse at decode; phase and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2449:exclusive custody itself, atomic admission, crash recovery, input\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The ancestry rule fixes the immediate closed-predecessor problem, but it leaves important constraints unstated: which ancestor IDs a successor may retry, whether dedup spans target streams, and how a halted install child differs from a halted root. I also confirmed the two reported implementation gaps by inspection; both affect version-0 handoff behavior.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1630,1740p'; rg -n '\"'^## 6|named but|M-next B'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md; rg -n 'phase|parent|response' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | head -25; rg -n 'Carriage|lapsed|transaction|author' docs/design/dao.lease.md | head -22; sed -n '626,655p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1260,1303p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n 'full|terminal|transport-error' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -15; sed -n '1500,1534p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1630,1740p'; rg -n '\"'^## 6|named but|M-next B'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md; rg -n 'phase|parent|response' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | head -25; rg -n 'Carriage|lapsed|transaction|author' docs/design/dao.lease.md | head -22; sed -n '626,655p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1260,1303p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n 'full|terminal|transport-error' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -15; sed -n '1500,1534p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"  1630\t### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n  1631\t\n  1632\t`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\n  1633\tnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\n  1634\tthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\n  1635\tincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\n  1636\tnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\n  1637\tlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\n  1638\teffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\n  1639\treplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\n  1640\tids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\n  1641\tLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\n  1642\tinstead:\n  1643\t\n  1644\t**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\n  1645\tledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\n  1646\t(7.7.3); only the possessing boundary writes it. Exactly-once admission\n  1647\tis available only at an enrolled transactional consumer. Its epoch check,\n  1648\toperation-intent check, durable dedup record, and side-effect commitment\n  1649\tshare one atomic boundary against the admission resource, never a remote\n  1650\tcheck followed by a local act. A stale epoch is refused at commitment.\n  1651\tA consumer unable to join that boundary declares at-least-once delivery\n  1652\tor fail-stop behavior; UCF does not infer exactly-once from a lease stamp.\n  1653\t\n  1654\t**Stable operation ids, intent, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect\n  1655\tcarries `:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}`, assigned in\n  1656\temission order. Re-grant may reuse an id after a nondeterministic input\n  1657\thas changed, such as a kept cursor returning `gap` after eviction. The\n  1658\tsame id therefore does not prove the same operation. An enrolled consumer\n  1659\tcompares the canonical intent (effect kind, target identity, and payload)\n  1660\twith its durable `{op-id -> {intent result}}` record in the atomic\n  1661\tadmission transition. An equal id and intent returns the recorded result\n  1662\twithout a second commit; an equal id with different intent refuses and\n  1663\tcommits nothing. A crash re-grant must replay durably recorded inputs or\n  1664\tfail-stop when a divergent intent cannot be reconciled. Without that\n  1665\tinput discipline and transactional enrollment, the composition declares\n  1666\tat-least-once behavior rather than exactly-once effects.\n  1667\t\n  1668\t**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\n  1669\tedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\n  1670\t**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\n  1671\tvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope ... :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\n  1672\t:yin.k/op-id ... :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. An enrolled\n  1673\tconsumer derives intent from the actual effect kind, destination identity,\n  1674\tand payload at admission. A bare stream is *declared* unprotected, and a\n  1675\tcomposition that needs exactly-once delivery through it has wired the\n  1676\twrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream; the resource enforces it.\n  1677\tNo callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\n  1678\tits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\n  1679\tthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\n  1680\tSection 7.7.8 publishes the envelope's grammar, which adds the epoch to\n  1681\tthe keys named here, and the sequence state behind the operation id.\n  1682\t\n  1683\t**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\n  1684\tcompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\n  1685\tthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\n  1686\tappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\n  1687\ttransition** -- observing the release (or the `:resumed` evidence followed\n  1688\tby release) and closing the occurrence's tenure. A crash after successor\n  1689\tpublication but before the ledger transition leaves an orphan successor,\n  1690\tnot a grant. The lease lapses and the grantor may re-grant the last\n  1691\trecorded occurrence. For enrolled consumers, durable input replay and\n  1692\tatomic intent comparison prevent a divergent same-id effect from\n  1693\tcommitting. Without those gates, the re-grant is at-least-once or\n  1694\tfail-stop, not harmless exactly-once recovery. The orphan remains\n  1695\tqueryable history (7.7.6); a holder report is evidence, while the grantor\n  1696\tledger is authority.\n  1697\t\n  1698\t**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\n  1699\tthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\n  1700\tcommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\n  1701\tdelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\n  1702\tproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\n  1703\tis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\n  1704\tarbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\n  1705\tafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\n  1706\tactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\n  1707\treclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\n  1708\tloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\n  1709\tand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\n  1710\tmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\n  1711\t`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\n  1712\tduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\n  1713\t\n  1714\t### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\n  1715\t\n  1716\tA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\n  1717\tagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\n  1718\tor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\n  1719\t`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\n  1720\toccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\n  1721\tpredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\n  1722\tappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\n  1723\taddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\n  1724\tis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\n  1725\tmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\n  1726\tre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\n  1727\trecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\n  1728\tare what compensation reads to find what was done.\n  1729\t\n  1730\t### 7.7.7 Restart\n  1731\t\n  1732\tA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1733\t*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\n  1734\tend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\n  1735\toccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\n  1736\t`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\n  1737\trecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\n  1738\twhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\n  1739\t\n  1740\t### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\n317:## 6. Pending amendments named but not landed\n319:- The stage-2 amendment of the post-M5 hardening (M-next B,\n75:stream boundary. This document makes the request and response a stream\n87:content on a response stream and is installed by a child evaluation the\n407:   correlated response on the content response stream (section 6). The\n529:     export is verified at run time in the `validated` phase of section\n536:     verified image in the response. The receiver checks each against\n783:  L -- \"link response stream\" --> K\n785:  J -- \"content response stream\" --> L\n803:The content pair is `dao.jing.content`'s request and response vocabulary\n886:refusal is appended on the response stream under the request's id where\n894:unsent content requests, poll the content response medium at most\n895:`budget` elements, route each correlated response to its link, run the\n896:verification step that response enables, issue the next content request\n946:every local `fetch` produces exactly the request/response traffic on the\n991:    S->>S: mint response cursor; :link-request entry\n993:    S->>S: append ok -> :link-response entry\n999:    L->>S: append link response {id, :ok image, obligations}\n1022:   response stream; then it builds the wait entry in its first state:\n1031:    :response  {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\n1036:   observing events caused by an operation: a response that lands\n1042:   share one response stream: the root task and every install child\n1054:4. **The request is appended; `ok` moves the entry to the response\n1057:   `{:reason :link-response :link-id [:t0 7] :response ... :cursor ...}`.\n1063:   needed to rebuild the envelope, `:link-response` carries the response\n1076:6. **The response is appended** to the link response stream with the\n1080:7. **`check-wait-set` polls each `:link-response` entry's kept cursor and\n34:| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`         |\n73:`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\n78:- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n81:- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n82:  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n134:and **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n160:   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n161:   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n175:6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n180:The record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n207:- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n241:  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n246:- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n283:## Carriage\n286:payload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n287:is delivery, not a second authoring.\n289:`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n299:- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n                                               (symbol (str (name m) \"/\"\n                                                            (name x))))\n                                             (get-in inst\n                                                     [:response :manifest\n                                                      :yin.module/exports])))\n                                      (:installs vm)))\n             missing (update (:yin.k/missing walked) :obligations\n                             #(set/difference (set %) installing))\n             discovery (cond\n                         (seq (:segments missing)) :blocked\n                         (some seq (vals (dissoc missing :segments)))\n                         :incomplete\n                         :else :complete)]\n         (when-not (= :complete discovery)\n           (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied\n                    {:yin.k/discovery discovery\n                     :yin.k/missing missing}))\n         (let [value (:value vm)\n               active (and (map? value)\n                           (= :parked-continuation (:type value))\n                           (contains? (:parked vm) (:id value)))\n               active-rec (when active (get (:parked vm) (:id value)))\n               kind (cond\n                      active :parked\n                      (engine/halted-with-empty-queue? vm) :halted\n                      (seq (:wait-set vm)) :blocked\n                      :else (not-at-safepoint!\n                              (get-in vm [:control :pc])))\n               _ (when (and (= :halted kind) (seq (:wait-set vm)))\n                   (non-portable! :inconsistent-halt {}))\n\n      (undecodable! {:yin.k/reason (:yin.k/reason pending)}))))\n\n\n(defn resume-task\n  \"The lower of 14.1.2: `bytes`, a handoff body this namespace minted,\n   resumed into a fresh task over `recv` -- a machine of the receiver's\n   own composition.  Answers `{:status :ok :kind k :vm resumed}` or the\n   first data refusal: `:yin.k/undecodable` bytes or grammar,\n   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` stamp or version, `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`\n   code, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` a stream that cannot be attached or a\n   primitive the receiver cannot answer.  Nothing partially runnable\n   is ever exposed: the machine value is assembled only after every\n   restoration step passes.  `attach!` is the receiver's own\n   `:dao.stream/attach` dispatch, exactly `yin.vm.ucf.remote`'s; the\n   resumed task is a fork until stage 2.\"\n  ([recv bytes attach!]\n   (resume-task recv bytes attach! nil))\n  ([recv bytes attach! opts]\n   (call!\n     (fn []\n       (let [body (try (cbor/decode bytes)\n                       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable\n                                 :cljs :default)\n                              _\n                         (undecodable! {:yin.k/kind :bytes})))\n             _ (validate-body body)\n             _ (when-not (= ucf/contract-stamp\n                            (:yin.k/contract body))\n                 (refuse! :yin.k/profile-mismatch\n                          {:yin.k/contract (:yin.k/contract body)}))\n             kind (:yin.k/kind body)\n             cells (:yin.k/cells body)\n             pendings (mapv :yin.k/pending\n                            (or (:yin.k/frames body) []))\n             recv' (reduce (fn [r [_a v]] (module/attach-module r v))\n                           recv\n                           (:yin.k/code body))\n             handles (attach-all! attach! (body-markers body))\n             used (atom (into fixed-resource-keys\n                              (keys (:resources recv))))\n             streams (into {}\n                           (map (fn [identity]\n                                  [identity (fresh-key used)]))\n129:  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n162:position holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n335:| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n344:| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n450:— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n475:spans the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n515:  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n520:  `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n580:| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n595:| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n599:| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport declares that its failures are clean.     | —             |\n612:`transport-error` means nothing was appended.\n617:`transport-error` whose effect is unknown is never retried automatically.\n678:Writing). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\n703:`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\n\nCustody is negotiated on an **arbitration medium** — a `dao.space` whose\nfacts are ordinary datoms — using `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged,\nwith the checkpoint **occurrence** as the leased subject. The UCF-authored\nfacts are dispatch-keyed exactly as lease facts are (`dao.lease.md`,\n*Vocabulary*: a reader switches on `:dao.lease/status`; a fact carrying it\nis a lease fact, one carrying neither is ignored):\n\n| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Required keys |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | emitter | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id` (this snapshot variant), `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium` (the carrier identity) |\n| checkpoint done | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | holder | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result` — the address of the successor's occurrence value or of a `:yin.k/result` |\n\nLease facts themselves keep their own table verbatim — proposal\n(`:dao.lease/proposed`, with `:dao.lease/subject = {:yin.k/occurrence O}`),\ngrant (`:dao.lease/accepted`, with `:dao.lease/lease :dao.lease/holder\n:dao.lease/subject :dao.lease/duration`, and `:dao.lease/max` optional),\nrefusal, release, renewal, lapse — each dispatched on `:dao.lease/status`\nor `:dao.lease/event`, each with `dao.lease.md`'s required keys, unchanged.\nA lease judge reading the same medium switches on the lease keys and\nignores the `:yin.k/custody` facts; a UCF reader switches on\n`:yin.k/custody` and reads lease facts for authority. Both `:yin.k/custody`\nfacts are **evidence, not authority**: an offer creates no claim, and a\n`:resumed` record is a holder's report — completion is the grantor's ledger\ntransition, not the report (§7.7.6). Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s:\n**only grantor-authored facts establish terms.**\n\nThe subject of every grant is the **occurrence**, never `:yin.k/id`:\nsnapshot variants and retries of one park share one occurrence, so no pair\nof encodings can hold independent grants, and an equal-content copy minted\nby coincidence is a different occurrence and a different computation\n(§7.3.4).\n\n### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
